  "result": "I wrote the 30-minute lesson for O3 at `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`. I haven't opened it in a browser or counted its words. I estimate about 3,300, which is inside the 2,700–6,750 range the bench accepts for 30 minutes. I didn't write a quiz.\n\n**What it covers:**\n- **Context window (5 min):** the model has no memory of its own, and everything it sees in a call is what your program puts there.\n- **Cost (4 min):** each call re-sends everything, so cost is total input tokens across all calls. A worked table gives 18,200 tokens for one request.\n- **Keeping context small (4 min):** shrink old tool results to a short stub, compact the history with care, and have tools return less.\n- **Memory between sessions (4 min):** exact facts like training status go in a store, and softer notes go in a notes file.\n- **Retrieval (4 min):** manuals are split into labeled chunks and fetched by a search tool, either up front or just in time.\n- **Warm-up, worked example and practice, summary (7.5 min):** the warm-up recalls the agent loop. The worked example estimates one visit at 8,200 tokens, and a task for you compares a \"load the whole manual\" design at 28,750.\n\nThe lesson has five practice checks with instant feedback and three figures. The main source is Anthropic's context engineering article.\n\nI also created `RESOURCES.md` and added working notes to `NOTES.md`. I didn't write a cheat sheet or a learning record, since you haven't shown understanding of this material yet.\n\nYou can ask me anything about the lesson in the chat, and `/quiz` will write the quiz when you want it.\n\nQuestion for you: do you want me to list communities, such as forums or clubs, for testing these ideas, or do you prefer your design team and the review as the only place?",
